        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How We Chose These Curved Treadmills</h2>
<p>
            We have not tested these machines and do not claim to have. The picks were chosen from
            published specifications, manufacturer listings, warranty terms and independent
            coverage — specialist review sites, dealer specification sheets and patterns that recur
            in owner feedback across more than one source.
          </p>
          <p>
            Three rules shaped the list. First, every machine had to be a current model from a
            maker that still lists it. The curved category has a lot of churn and a lot of
            short-lived budget listings, and stock is genuinely patchy at the top: on the day we
            checked, Assault Fitness showed all three AssaultRunner models sold out on its own
            store, and Woodway's shop showed the Curve LTG sold out, while specialist dealers still
            listed both. We include the Woodway because it is a current product rather than a
            discontinued one, and say on its card that availability needs checking.
          </p>
          <p>
            Second, we looked for budget curved models and did not find one under four figures we
            could stand behind. There are folding machines on Amazon for a few hundred dollars
            described as curved, but their specification sheets contradict each other, their decks
            are flat belts with a slight bow, and independent coverage either does not exist or warns
            about long-term reliability. The cheapest slatted curved trainer we include is SB
            Fitness's CT400 at about $1,595, and its listing leaves out the deck size and user
            capacity. That is the honest floor for this category, and the <a href="#price" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">price section</a> explains why.
          </p>
          <p>
            Third, figures had to agree. Where the manufacturer and an independent source gave the
            same running surface, capacity or weight, it appears on the card. Where they did not —
            the Woodway's exact weight, the Tru Grit's dimensions — we left it out, and where a
            seller's own figures conflict, as on the CT700's weight rating, the card says so. A blank
            in the comparison table below is deliberate.
          </p>
          <p>
            Some respected commercial machines are not here. Technogym's Skillmill, for example,
            was listed on Technogym's US store at $15,250 on 20 October 2026, far above this
            range, so it is mentioned rather than ranked; our <a href="/technogym-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Technogym guide</a> covers it, and our <a href="/woodway-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Woodway guide</a> covers the rest of
            the Curve range.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="price" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What Curved Treadmills Cost, and Why</h2>
<p>
            The prices on this page are high, and it is worth being clear about that up front.
            The cheapest machine here is about $1,600; most cost $2,100 to $2,600; the Pro 6
            Arcadia is about $4,500, and outside this list the built-to-order TrueForm Runner runs from
            about $6,200 to over $10,000 depending on how it is configured. For comparison, a capable motorised running treadmill with incline can be
            had for $1,500 to $2,000. A curved treadmill is not the economical choice.
          </p>
          <p>
            The money goes into three things. <strong>The slat deck</strong> is the big one: dozens
            of individual rubber-topped slats on a bearing track, rather than a belt over a board.
            The AssaultRunner Pro has 62 of them. Each slat and each bearing has to be made to a
            tolerance that lets the whole surface roll freely under a sprinting adult. <strong>The frame</strong> is the second: most of these machines weigh 260 to 300
            pounds or more because the frame has to hold a curve true under hard, uneven loading. <strong>Low volumes</strong> are the third: this is a niche category, built largely
            for gyms and sports facilities, and home versions inherit commercial costs.
          </p>
          <p>
            What you get back is durability and the absence of a motor. There is no drive motor or
            motor controller to fail, which is where many motorised treadmills eventually die, and
            Assault and Woodway both quote belt life of around 150,000 miles. Over a long
            ownership the running costs are low. That does not close the gap on day one, and none
            of these machines will save you money over a motorised treadmill unless you would
            otherwise have bought a premium one.
          </p>
          <p>
            If $1,600 is more than you want to spend, there is no good curved treadmill for less.
            The sensible alternatives are a motorised treadmill, or a flat manual walker if
            walking is the goal. Do not buy a $400 "curved" folding machine expecting the
            experience of the machines on this page.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="slats-and-mechanics" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Slat Belt, Curve Shape and How It Runs</h2>
<p>
            How the curve turns your stride into belt speed is covered in detail in the <a href="/curved-treadmill-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">curved treadmill guide</a>,
            so here we stick to the differences between these seven machines.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The running surface</h3>
          <p>
            Most curved trainers use slats: the SOLE C80, both SB Fitness models and the Woodway Curve
            LTG here, and the AssaultRunner Pro and TrueForm models off Amazon. Slats follow the curve cleanly, give a firm,
            slightly springy footing and can be replaced individually. The materials differ — Assault
            uses a TPU surface, Woodway a TPE slat belt it describes as softer than average,
            TrueForm offers rubber, turf, track and textured Naboso surfaces — and the feel differs
            with them, but there is no published standard that lets you compare softness between
            brands. If cushioning matters to you, it is worth trying one in a gym.
          </p>
          <p>
            Running-surface size varies more than you might expect. The SOLE C80's deck is 62 by
            19 inches and the Sunny SF-X7110's is 59 by 17.7 inches; the sellers list the CT700 at 66.6
            by 19 and the Pro 6 Arcadia at 69 by 17, and for comparison the AssaultRunner Pro's is 62
            by 17. For sprinting, a wider surface gives more margin for a stride that wanders, which
            the 19-inch SOLE handles best of the machines with figures confirmed by more than one
            source.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Curve depth</h3>
          <p>
            Curves are not all the same shape. A steeper curve makes the belt respond more
            aggressively to small changes in foot position, which suits sprinters and can feel
            twitchy at easy paces. A shallower one is calmer and easier to hold at a steady jog.
            Woodway quotes about 3% curvature for the Curve LTG, and TrueForm, which is not on Amazon,
            says its Trainer has the shallowest curve on the market. Most makers do not publish a comparable figure, which is a good reason to try
            before buying if you can.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Resistance and braking</h3>
          <p>
            Six of the seven have adjustable resistance: the SOLE C80, SB Fitness CT700 and Sunny
            SF-X7110 with eight magnetic levels each, the <a href="/tru-grit-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Tru Grit</a> Runner with six levels that its dealers
            group as running, walking and sled-push settings, the Pro 6 Arcadia with six and the
            CT400 with three. The Woodway, like the AssaultRunner Pro and TrueForm models, has no
            resistance setting: effort comes entirely from how hard you run. Resistance matters for sled-style pushes against the handrails and for making
            short sprints harder without running faster. It matters much less if you only want to
            run freely. None of these machines has a motorised brake; the belt slows when you ease
            back toward the bottom of the curve, which takes a few sessions to learn. The <a href="/treadmill-sprint-workout/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill sprint workout</a> page covers how to structure sessions on one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="footprint-and-warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Footprint, Weight and Warranty Compared</h2>
<p>
            Every machine here is long, heavy and permanent. None folds. Most are around 64 to 72
