            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Weight and delivery</h3>
          <p>
            At 260 to 300 pounds, most of these machines are a two- or three-person carry at best,
            and stairs can rule a room out on their own. The Sunny SF-X7110, at 157 pounds, is the
            exception and the main reason it is on this list. Ask the seller whether the machine
            arrives assembled, whether delivery is curbside or room-of-choice, and whether there is
            a charge for taking it upstairs. Floors need thinking about too: a sprinting adult on a
            300 lb machine puts real load and vibration into a suspended floor, so a ground floor,
            garage or basement is the natural home, ideally on a thick rubber mat.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Reading the warranty</h3>
          <p>
            The frame headline is the least useful number in that table. Curved trainer frames
            rarely fail; slats, bearings and consoles are what wear. So look past the frame term to
            the moving parts and labour. The SB Fitness CT700's 5 years on parts and the Woodway's
            3 years on the belt and moving parts are meaningfully stronger than the SOLE C80's single
            year on parts and labour or the Pro 6 Arcadia's single year on parts, even though the
            SOLE's lifetime frame term reads best. Several makers also restrict the home warranty to non-commercial use, so a
            machine used for paid personal training sessions at home may not be covered. Older
            reviews quote different terms for some of these models — Assault's frame warranty, for
            example, appears as 5 years in earlier coverage and 7 years on the current listing —
            so confirm which terms apply on the day you buy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="choosing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Choosing Between the Seven</h2>
<p>
            Most buyers can narrow the list with three questions, asked in this order.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Do you want resistance?</strong> If you plan sled-style pushes against the
            handrails, or want to make short sprints harder without running faster, the choice is
            is mostly between the SOLE C80, the SB Fitness CT700, the Tru Grit Runner and the Sunny
            SF-X7110. The Tru Grit is the most conditioning-focused; the SOLE has the widest
            confirmed deck and the strongest brand support; the CT700 has the longest parts warranty;
            the Sunny is the one to choose if the machine has to go up stairs. If
            you only want to run freely, resistance is a nice extra rather than a reason to choose.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>How much do you weigh, and how hard will you run?</strong> Heavier runners
            should start with the 350 lb machines: the SB Fitness CT550 and CT700, whose sellers give
            350 lb (the CT700's gives 350 to 475 lb in different places, so plan on 350). Off Amazon,
            the Woodway Curve LTG is rated at 350 lb and the TrueForm Trainer at 400 lb. Leave a margin rather than buying to the limit. For all-out
            sprinting, a heavier frame stays more planted, which favours the 260 to 300 lb machines
            over the 157 lb Sunny.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What is your ceiling on price and waiting time?</strong> Under about $1,600,
            the SB Fitness CT400 is the only option here. Under about $2,600, the SOLE C80, CT700,
            Tru Grit Runner, Sunny SF-X7110 and SB Fitness CT550 compete. At $3,500 to $4,600, the
            Pro 6 Arcadia Air Runner and, from Woodway's dealers rather than Amazon, the Woodway Curve
            LTG are the choices, and current stock may decide between them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Who a Curved Treadmill Is Wrong For</h2>
<p>
            The curved design is excellent at sprints, intervals and conditioning, and wrong for a
            surprising number of the reasons people buy treadmills. Be honest about which of these
            describes you before spending $2,000 or more. For a cheaper direct-sold option, see our page on the <a href="/sb-fitness-ct550-curved-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">SB Fitness CT550</a>.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Walkers who want incline.</strong> None of these machines offers incline
            in the way a motorised treadmill does, and walking slowly on a curve takes constant
            small adjustments to hold your place. If incline walking is your main use, an <a href="/best-incline-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">incline treadmill</a> will serve you far better for less money.</li>
            <li><strong>Small apartments and upper floors.</strong> At around 70 inches long, 260 to
            300 pounds, non-folding and noisy under a sprinting runner, a curved trainer is a poor
            fit for a flat with neighbours below. Our <a href="/best-treadmills-for-apartments/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">apartment treadmill guide</a> covers machines that suit those constraints.</li>
            <li><strong>Long, easy runners.</strong> Because you supply all the power, a steady
            easy pace feels harder than on a motorised belt, and holding it for an hour takes
            concentration. Runners who mainly log long easy miles often find a motorised treadmill
            more pleasant.</li>
            <li><strong>Anyone who wants to work, read or watch while walking.</strong> A curved
            trainer needs your attention. It is not a walking desk.</li>
            <li><strong>Beginners with balance concerns or recent injuries.</strong> The belt
            speeds up as you move forward and slows as you move back, which takes practice.
            Speak to a doctor or physical therapist first if you have any condition affecting
            balance, joints or heart, and use the handrails while you learn.</li>
            <li><strong>Buyers on a $500 to $1,500 budget.</strong> There is no good curved
            treadmill at that price. A motorised treadmill or a flat manual walker is the better
            use of the money.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If you are a runner who wants sprint and interval work at home, a coach or athlete
            setting up a training space, or someone who simply prefers driving the belt to chasing
            it, a curved trainer can be the best piece of equipment you own. That is who this list
            is for.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Common Mistakes Buying a Curved Treadmill</h2>
          <p>Each of these is expensive to correct on a machine that can weigh close to 300 lb.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Buying one to walk on</h3>
              <p>A curved trainer is a running and conditioning machine. Slow walking on a curve takes constant adjustment and there is no settable incline. If walking is your main use, a motorised incline treadmill does it better for less, and the $2,000-plus goes further elsewhere.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Not measuring stairs, doorways and ceiling height</h3>
              <p>Most curved trainers weigh 260 to 300 lb, are around 70 inches long and do not fold, and the deck sits high. Measure every doorway and turn on the route to the room, and check that a tall runner has headroom at the top of their stride, before ordering.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Judging the warranty by the frame term</h3>
              <p>Frames rarely fail; slats, bearings and consoles do. A lifetime frame with one year on parts can be weaker cover than five years on the frame with three on moving parts. Read the parts and labour terms, and check whether home use for paid sessions is excluded.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Assuming the advertised machine is in stock</h3>
              <p>Curved trainer stock moves around. On the day we checked, two leading makers showed their models sold out on their own stores while dealers listed them. Confirm availability and the lead time in writing before you plan around a delivery date or sell an existing machine.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            For most home runners, the <strong class="text-white">SOLE C80</strong> is the curved
            treadmill to buy: eight magnetic levels, a 19-inch deck and an established brand for about
            $2,200. The <strong class="text-white">SB Fitness CT700</strong> trades brand support for a far
            longer parts warranty, and the <strong class="text-white">CT400</strong> is the cheapest
            slatted trainer we found.
          </p>
          <p>
            Curved treadmills are expensive specialist tools. If you mostly walk, want incline or live
            in a small upper-floor flat, spend the money on a motorised machine instead — our <a href="/best-incline-treadmills/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">incline treadmill picks</a> are a better starting point. If you are unsure, the <a href="/curved-treadmill-guide/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">curved treadmill guide</a> will help you decide.
          </p>
        </div>

