        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Why are curved treadmills so expensive?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              The slat deck is the main cost: dozens of rubber-topped slats on a bearing track built to roll freely under a sprinting adult. Frames weigh 260 to 300 pounds to keep the curve true, and the category is low-volume and commercial in origin. The cheapest slatted curved trainer we include, the SB Fitness CT400, was about $1,595 on Amazon in November 2026.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Are cheap curved treadmills on Amazon any good?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              The few-hundred-dollar ones are not. Folding machines described as curved for a few hundred dollars usually have a flat belt with a slight bow, specification sheets that contradict each other and little independent coverage. They do not give the experience of a slatted curved trainer. The cheapest real slatted trainer we found on Amazon was the SB Fitness CT400 at about $1,595; below that, a motorised treadmill is the better buy.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can you walk on a curved treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Yes, but it is not what they do best. Walking slowly on a curve takes constant small adjustments to stay in place, and there is no incline to make it harder. Shallower curves, such as the Woodway Curve LTG's, which Woodway quotes at about 3%, are calmer. If walking is your main use, a motorised walking or incline treadmill suits it better.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Which curved treadmill is best for heavier runners?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Among the machines carded on this page, the SB Fitness CT550's seller gives 350 lb, and the SB Fitness CT700's seller gives 350, 400 and 475 lb in different places, so plan on 350. The SOLE C80, Tru Grit Runner and Sunny SF-X7110 are rated at 330 lb. Off Amazon, the Woodway Curve LTG is rated at 350 lb and the TrueForm Trainer at 400 lb. Choose a machine with clear headroom above your weight and confirm the rating with the seller before ordering.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Do curved treadmills need electricity?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              No. Every machine on this list is self-powered: you drive the belt. Consoles run on batteries or on power generated by the belt. That means a curved trainer can go in a garage, outbuilding or any spot without an outlet, and there is no drive motor or motor controller to fail over time.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Do curved treadmills have incline?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Not in the way motorised treadmills do. The curve itself creates a rising slope at the front, but you cannot set a 10% walk the way you can on an incline treadmill. If incline walking is the reason you are buying, a motorised incline treadmill is the right machine and costs less than most curved trainers.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

        <!-- Why trust us -->
        <section id="why-trust-us" class="ed-trust not-prose">
